Key Points to Remember
• Resource server vs authorization server: this app never sees a username or password. It only
checks the token's signature (using the public keys from the issuer), issuer, and expiry.
• 401 vs 403: no token or an invalid/expired token gives 401 (invalid_token); a valid token that lacks the
required scope gives 403 (insufficient_scope).
Practical No. 18 - OAuth2 Resource Server with JWT Page 8
• Scopes to authorities: Spring reads the scope claim and prefixes each value with SCOPE_, so scope
read is checked with hasAuthority('SCOPE_read').
• Stateless and no CSRF: the token is sent on every request, so no session is created and CSRF
protection is disabled.
• Deprecation note: the no-argument oauth2.jwt() is deprecated in Spring Security 6; use
oauth2.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()) as shown.
• issuer-uri vs jwk-set-uri: issuer-uri also validates the iss claim and discovers the key URL
automatically; jwk-set-uri only supplies the keys.
• Always use HTTPS in production; a Bearer token is a credential and anyone holding it can use it until
it expires.
Conclusion
The Spring Boot application was secured as an OAuth2 Resource Server. Every /api/secure/** request
must carry a valid JWT Bearer token: missing or invalid tokens are rejected with 401, tokens without the
required scope with 403, and valid tokens with the right scope can read, create, update and delete Notes
in H2.
